  "Level": "AMC 10",
  "Batch": "A",
  "Area": "Unclassified",
  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "Define \\(x \\heartsuit y\\) to be \\(|x - y|\\) for all real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y.\\) Which of the following statements is not true?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x \\heartsuit y = y \\heartsuit x\\) for all \\(x\\) and\n\\(y\\)",
    "B": "\\(2(x \\heartsuit y) = (2x) \\heartsuit (2y)\\) for all \\(x\\) and\n\\(y\\)",
    "C": "\\(x \\heartsuit 0 = x\\) for all \\(x\\)",
    "D": "\\(x \\heartsuit x = 0\\) for all \\(x\\)",
    "E": "\\(x \\heartsuit y \\gt 0\\) if \\(x \\ne y\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The definition gives \\(x\\heartsuit0=|x|\\). A negative input therefore tests statement C: for example,\n\\[\n(-1)\\heartsuit0=|-1-0|=1\\ne-1.\n\\]\nThis single counterexample disproves the claim that \\(x\\heartsuit0=x\\) for every real \\(x\\).\n\nThe remaining statements hold. For A, \\(x-y\\) and \\(y-x\\) are opposites, so their absolute values are equal. For B, doubling a number doubles its absolute value, giving \\(|2x-2y|=2|x-y|\\). For D, \\(|x-x|=0\\). For E, \\(x\\ne y\\) makes \\(x-y\\) nonzero, so \\(|x-y|>0\\).\n\nThus the statement that is not true is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
